# Each phrase promises a change for a later version, which the docs
# cannot keep current. The issue tracker holds the plan, and the docs
# describe what ships.
#
# Four phrases keep a runtime sense behind a guard. `in the future`
# skips a form of `be`, `have`, or a time noun before it (`the
# timestamp must be in the future`, `values we might have in the
# future`, `two years in the future`), and a glued `?` after it (`Is
# the date in the future?`). The filter narrows the finding to the
# phrase itself, and a glued comma has its own branch so the sentence
# opener (`In the future, ...`) still counts once. That opener skips
# `whenever`, `when`, `if`, or `you` after the comma, because a tutorial
# uses it for the reader's own next steps (`In the future, whenever you
# push a change, the site rebuilds`). `we` still counts there, because
# `In the future, we plan to add` is a promise.
#
# `will be added` skips `to` after it, where it says what the code does
# with a value (`all headers will be added to the map`). That guard also
# drops `no new features will be added to cgroup v1`, a promise the rule
# misses. `is planned` skips an agent, the actor named after `by` (`the
# query is planned by the optimizer`). Both have a clause-end twin,
# because a negated word after a space needs a word to consume. `to be
# implemented` needs `yet`, `still`, or `remains` before it, because the
# phrase alone names a capability (`allows a filesystem to be
# implemented in user space`).
#
# It hit 94 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 14 of 20 sampled hits
# promised a later change. Most misses were the reader's own future
# (`crates you might use in the future`) or a tutorial step (`Going
# forward, let's add the package`).
expr when [be, is, are, was, were, have, time, date, point, timestamp, deadline, cutoff, moment, instant, far, further, year, years]
expr tutorial [whenever, when, if, you]
expr main [(going forward), <(!@when in the future![,, ?]), (in the future)>, (in the future, !@tutorial), (in a future release), (in a future version), (in an upcoming release), (in upcoming releases), (in future releases), (in future versions), (will be added !to), (will be added[., ;, :, (,)]), (will be supported), (will be removed), (will be deprecated), ([is, are] planned !by), ([is, are] planned!( )), (on the roadmap), (coming soon), (not yet implemented), (not yet supported), ([yet, still, remains, remain] to be implemented), (will soon), (soon be)]
let message "Roadmap promise. Describe the system as it ships, such as `the flag is deprecated`, and keep plans in the issue tracker."
let description "Flags phrases that promise a change for a later version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "The timestamp must be in the future."
allows "A since cutoff in the future excludes the row."
allows "The query is planned by the optimizer."
allows "The library allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space."
allows "In the future, whenever you push a change, GitHub Pages rebuilds the site."
allows "All headers will be added to the map."
